WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
Pattern 2: streaming to a browser.

2
00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:13.741
With streaming, the API sends the reply as it is generated, using server-sent events (SSE), a plain HTTP response with content type text/event-stream.

3
00:00:14.021 --> 00:00:21.808
The body is a sequence of events, each a few lines of field: value (most often event: and data: ), separated by a blank line.

4
00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:26.547
Lines that start with a colon are comments, which servers use as keep-alives.

5
00:00:26.827 --> 00:00:32.992
The format is defined in the HTML standard, and MDN’s guide is the practical reference.

6
00:00:33.272 --> 00:00:34.957
A chat feature streams twice.

7
00:00:35.237 --> 00:00:41.211
First hop: your server opens a stream to the Claude API and receives events in a fixed order.

8
00:00:41.491 --> 00:00:57.107
message_start arrives first, then for each content block a content_block_start, a run of content_block_delta events carrying the text, and a content_block_stop; then message_delta (which carries the stop reason and usage) and finally message_stop.

9
00:00:57.387 --> 00:01:09.696
The stream can also contain ping events, and, importantly, error events: the streaming docs show an overloaded_error arriving mid-stream, after the HTTP status was already 200.

10
00:01:09.976 --> 00:01:18.253
Second hop: your server re-emits the parts the user needs (text deltas, a final status) as its own event stream to the browser.

11
00:01:18.533 --> 00:01:21.264
Don’t forward the provider’s raw stream to the browser.

12
00:01:21.544 --> 00:01:22.568
Translate it.

13
00:01:22.848 --> 00:01:34.752
You decide the event vocabulary (for example meta, delta, done, error ), you drop what the client shouldn’t see, and you get a place to enforce deadlines and handle failures, which lesson 2 builds on.

14
00:01:35.032 --> 00:01:36.589
Three practical details.

15
00:01:36.869 --> 00:01:39.237
The SDK’s stream helpers ( client.

16
00:01:39.517 --> 00:01:40.435
messages.

17
00:01:40.715 --> 00:01:41.568
stream(.)

18
00:01:41.848 --> 00:01:47.523
with text_stream and get_final_message() in Python) do the parsing for you on the first hop.

19
00:01:47.803 --> 00:02:01.435
The browser’s built-in EventSource only issues GET requests without a body, so chat front ends that need to POST a message often use fetch and read the response body as a stream instead; the wire format is the same.

20
00:02:01.715 --> 00:02:03.763
And over HTTP/1.

21
00:02:04.043 --> 00:02:15.819
1, browsers allow only about six concurrent SSE connections per domain across all tabs, according to MDN, so long-lived streams per tab add up faster than you’d expect.
